mode: "100644"
text: "# Where Community Lives\n\nCommunity can grow in several places. Choosing the right one — by **where your audience already is** and\n**a clear purpose**, not by platform hype — is the decision that makes or breaks it.\n\n> Re-verify platform features/stats quarterly.\n\n## The spectrum\n\nFrom lightest-touch to most-owned:\n\n1. **In-feed comment culture** — members talking in your comments, DMs, and shares. The starting point\n   for everyone; you can build real community here without any new platform (facilitate member-to-member\n   replies, pin great ones, ask questions back). Most brands should **max this out first.**\n2. **Semi-owned spaces** — **Instagram/WhatsApp Broadcast Channels, Close Friends, group chats.** A\n   direct line to your most engaged fans, outside the feed algorithm. Treat them as **VIP spaces**\n   (exclusive drops, behind-the-scenes, early access) — **not promo dumps.** DMs increasingly outpace\n   feeds for real conversation.\n3. **Owned community spaces** — **Facebook/LinkedIn Groups, Discord, Slack, Substack, Telegram,\n   newsletters.** Built for peer-to-peer interaction and durable, algorithm-proof relationships.\n   Highest potential, **highest commitment.**\n\n## Choose by audience + purpose (not by hype)\n\n**The gate:** don't launch a Discord because a competitor has one. Ask:\n\n- **Where is your audience already gathering and comfortable?** Pick that. (Professional/interest-led →\n  LinkedIn/Facebook Groups; highly engaged fan/creator/dev communities → Discord; written depth →\n  Substack/newsletter; UGC-driven → IG/TikTok.) \"The right choice is guided by audience behavior, not\n  platform popularity.\"\n- **Is there a clear promise to join?** A space without a reason to exist goes **noisy, inactive, or\n  promo-dependent** fast. Define what members gain (early access, expert help, networking, learning,\n  belonging) before opening a single channel.\n- **Can you commit?** An owned space is an **ongoing program**, not a launch. It needs consistent\n  presence and **moderation from day one** (trained mods, clear rules, escalation → `crisis-and-moderation`).\n\n## Start small and earned\n\n- **Begin in-feed**, prove members want to connect, then graduate the most engaged into a semi-owned or\n  owned space. Don't build a big empty room.\n- **One owned space, done well** beats three half-alive ones. Concentrate where the community actually\n  is (echoes the engagement-routine \"concentrate, don't spread thin\" rule).\n- **The #1 mistake:** treating an owned space like a broadcast channel. Members want **participation,\n  access, and each other** — if it's an announcement feed, retention drops.\n\n## Examples of the shape\n\nBranded groups (e.g. a 40k-member Facebook Group around a product), VIP broadcast channels for content\ndrops, layered Discords (open rooms + focused channels + gated superfan sections + voice), and\nfan-to-fan frameworks (rituals/identity markers that let fans connect with *each other*). The common\nthread: the brand creates the **space and the reasons**, the **members create the community.**\n"
